Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi
Chris,

After the webinar I
compared using HSCV and AICc as the IC within ctmm.select() as you
suggested after hearing about what I have seen with some models that included a
3m error term within GUESS. I did not explore IC=LOOCV as my
goal for these analyses is to derive a home range. Using HSCV seems promising.
But I did notice that after fitting movement models and attempting to extract
the top model, the extracted model was not what was listed first in the summary
of candidate models. For instance, the following eight models extracted for an
individual do not match the sequence seen in the summary.

#1[1] "OU isotropic error"

#2[1] "OUf isotropic error"

#3[1] "OUF isotropic error"

#4 [1] "IID isotropic error"

#5[1] "OUF anisotropic error"

#6[1] "BM isotropic error"

#7[1] "OUF anisotropic error"

#8[1] "BM isotropic error"

Models in the summary
are listed by AIC but that is not the information criterion used with HSCV, so
I'm wondering if that has something to do with this. If what I'm seeing is
correct, is it appropriate to use the first model extracted from the candidate
set regardless of what order is shown in the summary? For many individuals IID
was the extracted model with topmodel <- FIT[[1]] but it did not appear first in the
summary. I could usually get another movement model as the "top"
model if I fiddled with GUESS enough. If the second extracted model
is a relatively good fit, would it be better to go with that over IID? Thanks
as always for the guidance!

Ross

